{0 Inlay hints (link direction)}

Next to every link whose target lies in the {e same note}, show which way
the target is and how far: [{v [[#setup]]↓8 v}].

A note that links inward a lot — a map-of-content, a long note that
cross-references its own sections — reads as a list of identical
[ [[#…]] ] tokens.  The one thing a reader wants from such a list, and
cannot get without following each link, is where each target sits
relative to the link.  That is a number the index already knows.

This is a separate feature from
{!page-"feature-codelens-reference-counts"}, which counts inbound
references at headings and at the top of the file.  The two answer
different requests and share nothing but the sense of being ambient
annotation, and each has its own switch: a reader who wants direction
arrows on links is not thereby asking for [3 refs] on every heading.

{1 Activation}

Requested with the rest of the inlay hints, over the client's visible
range.  Off switches the whole feature:

{t
  | Key                        | Type      | Default |
  |----------------------------|-----------|---------|
  | [inlayHints.linkDirection] | [boolean] | [true]  |
}

{1:eligible Which links get a hint}

Exactly those whose target resolves to a position {e within the current
note}:

{ul
  {- [ [[#Heading]] ], [ [[#^blockid]] ], [ [[#id]] ] — the self-reference
     forms, resolving to [Curr_heading], [Curr_block], [Curr_attr].}
  {- the same fragments written through the note's own name
     ([ [[this-note#Heading]] ] from inside [this-note.md]).}
  {- markdown links spelled [(#fragment)], which resolve identically.
     See {!page-"feature-go-to-definition".resolution}.}
}

Everything else is silent — no hint, not an empty one:

{ul
  {- {b cross-note links}: there is no direction to show.}
  {- {b unresolved links}, and links to a note whose fragment did not
     resolve: nothing to point at.  Reporting them is
     {!page-"feature-diagnostics"}'s job.}
  {- {b whole-note self-links} ([ [[this-note]] ] with no fragment): the
     target is the note itself rather than a place in it.}
}

Embeds ([![[#section]]]) are links for this purpose and get a hint.

{1:placement Placement}

The hint sits {e immediately after} the link's last character — after
[\]\]] for a wikilink, after the closing [)] for a markdown link — with
[paddingLeft].  Not at the end of the line.

This is what makes a line with two links legible.  End-of-line placement
would have to merge them into one label ([↓12 ↑3]) and leave the reader
to map arrows onto links by document order, silently and wrongly the
moment the line wraps.  Adjacent placement is self-labelling: [n] links
produce [n] hints, each beside its own.

{[
  See [[#alpha]]↓12 and [[#bar]]↑3 here.
]}

Two links to the {e same} target on one line each still get a hint.
Suppressing the second would make a hint depend on its neighbours, which
is the end-of-line problem returning by another door.

{1:label Label}

Let [Δ] be [target_line - hint_line], both 0-based, where [hint_line] is
the line the hint is placed on.

{t
  | Case                                | Label   |
  |-------------------------------------|---------|
  | [Δ > 0] (target below)              | [↓{Δ}]  |
  | [Δ < 0] (target above)              | [↑{-Δ}] |
  | [Δ = 0], target starts after the link | [→]   |
  | [Δ = 0], target starts before the link | [←]   |
  | [Δ = 0], target spans the link      | none    |
}

The arrow carries the sign, so the number is always the absolute
distance in lines: [↑45], never [↑-45].

Left and right are only reachable through inline attribute anchors
([ [key]{#kt} ]) — headings and block anchors own their line, so a
same-line target cannot be one.  There the line delta is [0] and the
number is dropped: the arrow alone says it.  The final row is the
degenerate case of a link that sits inside its own target's span; it
would point at itself, so it says nothing.

{1 Hint shape}

{t
  | Field       | Value                              |
  |-------------|------------------------------------|
  | position    | first position after the link      |
  | label       | see {!label}                       |
  | kind        | [InlayHintKind.Parameter]          |
  | paddingLeft | [true]                             |
}

Positions are UTF-16 (see {!page-"feature-utf16-positions"}), so a link
after non-ASCII text on the same line still gets its hint in the right
place.

{1 Edge cases}

{ul
  {- {b Link spanning lines}: [Δ] is measured from the line the hint
     lands on — the last line of the link — so the arrow means what it
     says at the place it appears.}
  {- {b Range}: a hint is emitted when {e the link} falls in the
     requested range.  The target may be anywhere in the note, on screen
     or not.}
  {- {b Unsaved edits}: inlay hints are a disk-reading handler, so an
     arrow is computed against the file as last saved.  A dirty buffer
     can therefore be off by the number of lines the edit added above
     the target — the known staleness of the whole disk group, not a
     separate defect here, and it resolves on save.  See
     {!page-"feature-document-sync"}.  Direction arrows are the disk
     group's most position-sensitive member, so if that split is ever
     revisited this is the feature that should motivate it.}
}
